      "html": "<div class=\"post__content post__content--new-editor\"><p>Did you mean <strong>Wokwi</strong>: <a href=\"https://wokwi.com/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow ugc\" target=\"_blank\">https://wokwi.com/</a> ?</p><p> </p><p>Documentation is here: <a href=\"https://docs.wokwi.com/?utm_source=wokwi\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow ugc\" target=\"_blank\">https://docs.wokwi.com/?utm_source=wokwi</a></p><p>Community: <a href=\"https://discord.com/invite/e5yFaayXkK\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow ugc\" target=\"_blank\">https://discord.com/invite/e5yFaayXkK</a></p><p>Youtubes: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/wokwi\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow ugc\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/wokwi</a></p><p> </p><blockquote><p>suggest best (sic) simulator for STM32F446RE</p></blockquote><p>There is no such thing as “best” - you’ll need to describe what you want from it before anyone can suggest what fits that requirement.</p><p> </p><p>Rather than simulate, why not just use an actual device?</p><p>A <a href=\"https://www.st.com/en/evaluation-tools/nucleo-f446re.html\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener nofollow ugc\" target=\"_blank\">NUCLEO-F446RE</a> is inexpensive, and comes with built-in ST-Link - so you have full visibility and control of the code running in the chip.</p></div>"
